From: Zhiyu722 <Zhiyu722@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 15:03:13 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 62/73] docs: audio all-silence root cause, vendor slimming
 attempt, BPF/GSI assessment

* section 13: reproduce and explain the "after a while every sound dies" bug.
  Playing three rounds of media makes the HAL's speaker-protection capture fail
  ("SM6150 ASM Loopback: ASoC: no backend DAIs enabled"): the ASM Loopback DPCM
  front end has no backend DAI attached because the vendor mixer path only sets
  TERT_MI2S_RX_VI_FB_MUX, never an FE->BE control.  The HAL's iv_feedback_count
  then never returns to zero, disable_snd_device(speaker) refuses to tear the
  route down and the speaker path sticks until reboot.  Both iv_feedback_count
  and spkr_prot live in the closed-source HAL, so the kernel can only make that
  path succeed or stop it from being taken.
* section 14 + docs/vendor-mod-attempt.md: slimming /vendor.  The factory image
  carries the shared_blocks dedup feature (read-only for Linux), so it was
  rebuilt from a directory tree with mke2fs -d; result is byte-identical to the
  factory image apart from the 26 deleted dataapp APKs and one build.prop line.
  Flashing it hung on the first boot screen (verity and/or an interrupted write)
  and was rolled back to the factory vendor image.
* section 15 + docs/bpf-gsi-feasibility.md: what running an Android 15/16 GSI
  would take.  AOSP enforces kernel 5.4 in the bpf loader (Connectivity change
  3285058); this tree already has all eight defconfig options the community
  checklist asks for, but none of the 5.x BPF machinery (BTF, ringbuf,
  sk_storage, bpf_link, JMP32, new prog types, the 5.x verifier).  Measured
  reference: 1444 commits for a comparable 4.19 device.

Signed-off-by: Zhiyu722 <Zhiyu722@users.noreply.github.com>
